======================================================================
  2. CONNECTION TO WALLIS PRODUCT AND pi
======================================================================

  n= 3: f=0.500000, 1/f^2=4.0000, 1/(f^2*k)=4.000000 (pi=3.14159)
  n= 4: f=0.375000, 1/f^2=7.1111, 1/(f^2*k)=3.555556 (pi=3.14159)
  n= 5: f=0.312500, 1/f^2=10.2400, 1/(f^2*k)=3.413333 (pi=3.14159)
  n= 6: f=0.273438, 1/f^2=13.3747, 1/(f^2*k)=3.343673 (pi=3.14159)
  n= 7: f=0.246094, 1/f^2=16.5120, 1/(f^2*k)=3.302394 (pi=3.14159)
  n= 8: f=0.225586, 1/f^2=19.6506, 1/(f^2*k)=3.275101 (pi=3.14159)
  n= 9: f=0.209473, 1/f^2=22.7901, 1/(f^2*k)=3.255722 (pi=3.14159)
  n=10: f=0.196381, 1/f^2=25.9300, 1/(f^2*k)=3.241252 (pi=3.14159)
  n=11: f=0.185471, 1/f^2=29.0703, 1/(f^2*k)=3.230036 (pi=3.14159)
  n=12: f=0.176197, 1/f^2=32.2109, 1/(f^2*k)=3.221089 (pi=3.14159)
  n=13: f=0.168188, 1/f^2=35.3516, 1/(f^2*k)=3.213785 (pi=3.14159)
  n=14: f=0.161180, 1/f^2=38.4925, 1/(f^2*k)=3.207710 (pi=3.14159)

  f(n)^2 * (n-2) -> 1/pi as n -> inf
  THE FIBER FRACTION IS THE WALLIS INTEGRAL!
  f(n) = (1/pi) * integral_0^pi sin^(2(n-2))(t) dt = beta function

