======================================================================
  6. RISING AND FALLING FACTORIAL CONNECTIONS
======================================================================

  n=3: (1/2)_1/k! = 1/2 = 0.500000, C(2k,k)/4^k = 1/2, match=True
  n=4: (1/2)_2/k! = 3/8 = 0.375000, C(2k,k)/4^k = 3/8, match=True
  n=5: (1/2)_3/k! = 5/16 = 0.312500, C(2k,k)/4^k = 5/16, match=True
  n=6: (1/2)_4/k! = 35/128 = 0.273438, C(2k,k)/4^k = 35/128, match=True
  n=7: (1/2)_5/k! = 63/256 = 0.246094, C(2k,k)/4^k = 63/256, match=True
  n=8: (1/2)_6/k! = 231/1024 = 0.225586, C(2k,k)/4^k = 231/1024, match=True
  n=9: (1/2)_7/k! = 429/2048 = 0.209473, C(2k,k)/4^k = 429/2048, match=True

  BEAUTIFUL: f(n) = (1/2)_(n-2) / (n-2)!

  The fiber fraction is the Pochhammer symbol (1/2)_k / k!
  where k = n-2.

  This is the coefficient of x^k in the Taylor expansion of
  (1-x)^(-1/2) = sum_k C(2k,k)/4^k * x^k

  So: f(n) = [x^(n-2)] (1-x)^(-1/2)

  THE GENERATING FUNCTION:
  sum_n f(n) * x^(n-2) = 1/sqrt(1-x)

  The fiber fraction sequence IS the Taylor coefficients
  of the inverse square root function!

